## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis for dynamic frequency tracking, demonstrates how to monitor hot keys efficiently, and contrasts incremental updates with full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized analysis for batched updates
- Frequency buckets for O(1) max-frequency queries
- Hash maps for per-label counts
- Sparse vs. dense label domains
- Space-time tradeoff analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
class HotKeyTracker {
  constructor(initialCounts) {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.maxFreq = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(initialCounts)) {
      this.cnt.set(k, v);
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, v);
    }
  }

  update(label, delta) {
    const old = this.cnt.get(label) || 0;
    const freqCount = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
    if (freqCount === 1) this.freq.delete(old);
    else this.freq.set(old, freqCount - 1);

    const nw = old + delta;
    this.cnt.set(label, nw);
    this.freq.set(nw, (this.freq.get(nw) || 0) + 1);
    this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, nw);
  }

  hasHotKey(threshold) {
    return this.maxFreq >= threshold;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical implementation of order-statistics data structures with batched updates. It expects a solution that balances fast batch updates with O(1) median queries by maintaining the median explicitly.

## Key Concepts
- Two balanced structures (lower and upper halves) to partition the data
- Lazy/deferred deletions: mark elements as deleted rather than removing them immediately, deferring cleanup to future operations
- Amortized analysis for batch updates and overall complexity
- Trade-offs between simple heaps and BST-based structures

## Code Example
```python
import heapq

class MedianTracker:
    def __init__(self):
        self.low = []   # max-heap (negatives)
        self.high = []  # min-heap
        self.deleted = set()

    def _cleanup(self):
        while self.low and -self.low[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(-heapq.heappop(self.low))
        while self.high and self.high[0] in self.deleted:
            self.deleted.remove(heapq.heappop(self.high))

    def insert(self, x):
        if not self.low or x <= -self.low[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, x)
        self._rebalance()
        self._cleanup()

    def delete(self, x):
        self.deleted.add(x)
        self._cleanup()

    def _rebalance(self):
        if len(self.low) < len(self.high):
            heapq.heappush(self.low, -heapq.heappop(self.high))
        elif len(self.low) - len(self.high) > 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.high, -heapq.heappop(self.low))

    def median_at_least(self, T):
        self._cleanup()
        return -self.low[0] >= T if self.low else False
```

